'use strict';
const KEY='prep-studio-v1';
const EMPTY=()=>({done:[],saved:[],days:[],notes:{},known:[],last:'1',theme:null});
const cards=[
 ['What changes when you assign b = a, where a is a list?','Both names refer to the same list. Mutating through either name changes that object. Rebinding b to a new list leaves a unchanged.','1'],
 ['When should you use is rather than ==?','Use == for value equality. Use is for identity, commonly when checking value is None. Do not rely on identity for string or numeric equality.','1'],
 ['What are -7 // 2 and -7 % 2?','They are -4 and 1. Integer // floors toward negative infinity; a = (a // b) * b + a % b.','2'],
 ['Do and / or always return True or False?','No. They short-circuit and return one of their operands. 0 or 5 returns 5; "a" and 7 returns 7. not always returns a Boolean.','2'],
 ['Why is a list a dangerous default argument?','Defaults are evaluated once at function definition, so the same list is shared across calls. Use None, then create a fresh list inside the function.','3'],
 ['What is a lambda, and when do you use it as a sort key?','A lambda is an anonymous one-expression function: lambda p: (p[0], -p[1]). key= sorts by that returned value in ascending order. A tuple is compared left to right; negating a number reverses that field. Prefer key=len over a lambda that only calls len.','3'],
 ['Does an if block create a local scope?','No. Neither if nor for introduces a separate local scope. A branch-only assignment may leave a name undefined when the branch is skipped.','4'],
 ['How do you include index 0 while iterating backward?','range(n - 1, -1, -1). The stop is excluded, so -1 is the bound that includes 0. Use reversed(values) if indices are unnecessary.','5'],
 ['How do out-of-range indexing and slicing differ?','An invalid sequence index raises IndexError. Slice bounds are clipped. A string/list slice produces a new sequence; a length-k slice generally costs O(k).','6'],
 ['How do sort() and sorted() differ?','list.sort() changes the list in place and returns None. sorted(iterable) returns a new list. Both support key and reverse, and both are stable.','7'],
 ['Why is [[0] * 3] * 2 risky?','Both outer entries refer to the same inner list. Mutating one row changes both. Use [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)] for independent rows.','7'],
 ['What can be a dictionary key or set element?','A hashable object. Integers and strings work; lists, dictionaries, and sets do not. A tuple works only when all of its elements are hashable.','8'],
 ['How do set and frozenset differ?','A set is mutable and unhashable. A frozenset is immutable and hashable when its elements are, so it can be a dict key or a set element. remove raises KeyError if missing; discard does not.','8'],
 ['How do you drop duplicates and keep first-seen order?','set(values) drops duplicates but does not guarantee order. list(dict.fromkeys(values)) keeps the first occurrence because dicts preserve insertion order.','8'],
 ['What does key in d check, and does d.get(key) insert?','Membership checks keys, not values. get returns the existing value or a default without inserting. Typical dictionary membership is O(1) average; scanning d.values() is O(n).','9'],
 ['How do Counter and a regular dict treat a missing key?','d["z"] raises KeyError. Counter returns 0 and does not insert the key. Use most_common for ranked frequencies, and Counter(s) == Counter(t) for anagrams.','9'],
 ['When does defaultdict create a value, and when does it not?','Missing-key access with [] calls the factory and stores the result, so defaultdict(list)["a"].append(0) works. .get("a") returns None and does not create a list.','9'],
 ['When do else and finally run in a try block?','else runs only if try finishes with no exception. finally runs on success or failure, including before a pending return. An unhandled exception still goes through finally.','11'],
 ['Why is return inside finally dangerous?','It overrides an earlier return and can swallow an exception raised in try. Use finally for cleanup only. raise means the function cannot finish normally; return means it did.','11'],
 ['Why is arr = arr.sort() wrong?','sort mutates the list and returns None, so that assignment throws the list away. Call arr.sort() as a statement, or use arr = sorted(arr).','i1'],
 ['How do you sort first ascending and second descending?','pairs.sort(key=lambda p: (p[0], -p[1])). Python sorts the keys ascending, so -p[1] makes larger original seconds come first. Negation is for numbers; it does not work for strings.','i1'],
 ['How do merge sort and quicksort differ?','Merge sort always takes O(n log n), is stable, and uses O(n) extra space. Quicksort is faster on average but O(n²) with a bad pivot, typically not stable, and uses O(log n) stack space. Python sort is Timsort, a stable mergesort of runs.','i1'],
 ['When is a linear-time sort allowed?','When you are not limited to pairwise comparisons: counting sort is O(n + k) for integers in 0..k; radix sort walks digits. A comparison sort cannot beat O(n log n) in the worst case. Insertion sort is O(n) only on nearly sorted data.','i1'],
 ['Why is list.pop(0) the wrong queue?','Removing from the front of a list is O(n). Use collections.deque: append to enqueue, popleft to dequeue, both O(1).','i4'],
 ['What is the difference between queue, dequeue, and deque?','A queue is FIFO. Enqueue adds; dequeue removes. A deque (said “deck”) is the double-ended structure. Use append/popleft for a queue and append/pop for a stack. BFS needs a queue so nodes are processed in discovery order.','i4'],
 ['What happens if you mutate a nested list in a shallow copy of [1, [2, 3]]?','The outer lists are separate, but they share the inner list. Changing copy[1][0] also changes original. deepcopy duplicates nested structure.','i3'],
 ['Why is [[0] * 3] * 2 the wrong 2-D grid?','Both rows refer to the same inner list. Use [[0] * 3 for _ in range(2)] so each row is independent.','i5'],
 ['When do you use defaultdict or Counter?','Frequencies of an iterable: Counter. Grouping: defaultdict(list). Counting in a loop: defaultdict(int) or Counter. Unique neighbors: defaultdict(set). [] inserts on defaultdict; Counter returns 0 and does not insert.','i6'],
 ['How do you get a max-heap from heapq?','heapq is a min-heap. Push and pop negated numbers, then negate the result. For custom order, store (priority, value) tuples.','i7'],
 ['What is sift-up vs sift-down, and why is heapify O(n)?','Insert sifts up from the end. Removing the root sifts down from index 0. heapify sifts down from the last parent toward the root, which is O(n), not O(n log n). A heap is only partially ordered; heap[0] is the extreme.','i7'],
 ['How do you find the k largest values with a heap?','Keep a min-heap of size k. The root is the smallest of the current winners, so pop it when a larger candidate arrives. Time O(n log k), space O(k). K smallest uses a size-k max-heap.','i7'],
 ['What can you use as a visited-cell key?','A hashable tuple such as (r, c). Lists are unhashable and cannot be dict keys or set elements.','i6'],
 ['When is bisect the stand-in for a sorted map?','If sortedcontainers is unavailable, keep a sorted list and use bisect to find the index in O(log n). Inserting into that list is still O(n).','i8'],
 ['What problems need a sorted map instead of a dict or heap?','Next key >= x (bisect_left), closest key (compare both neighbors of the insertion point), and sliding-window median (keep the window ordered so the middle is the median). A dict has no order; a heap only gives min/max.','i8'],
 ['What does min(vals, key=lambda v: (abs(v - x), v)) return?','The original candidate with the smallest distance, then the smaller value on a tie. key= is only for comparison. min of the tuples themselves would return (1, 3) instead of 3. Equal distances without v in the key keep whichever candidate appeared first.','i8'],
 ['Why is arr[i] O(1)?','The element lives at address base + i * size. The CPU computes that address and reads it. No scan from the start. A linked-list get(i) has no such formula and must walk i pointers.','d1'],
 ['What does a Python list of integers actually store?','A contiguous array of pointers to int objects, not the integers themselves. Indexing is still O(1) because those pointers have a fixed size, so the same address formula applies.','d1'],
 ['Why is inserting at index 1 in a static array O(n)?','Every element from that index onward must shift one slot right, or the new value would overwrite a live element. Shift from the end backward. Insert at the end is O(1) if capacity remains.','d2'],
 ['Why does doubling make append amortized O(1)?','Resizes copy 1 + 2 + 4 + … + n/2 < n elements across n appends, so total copy work is O(n) and the average per append is O(1). Growing by 1 each time copies 0+1+…+n = O(n²). A single resize is still O(n) that call.','d3'],
 ['Why is stack.pop(0) the wrong pop?','A stack pops the top, which is the end of a Python list: pop(). pop(0) removes the bottom and shifts everything, O(n). Peek with stack[-1] only after if stack:.','d4'],
 ['Why is get(i) on a singly linked list O(n)?','There is no index formula. You start at head and follow next i times. Insert at head is O(1) because you already hold that pointer.','d5'],
 ['How do you reverse 1 → 2 → 3, and what do you save first?','prev, cur, nxt. Save nxt = cur.next before flipping cur.next = prev, then walk prev = cur, cur = nxt. Return prev as the new head. Forgetting nxt loses the rest of the list.','d5'],
 ['Why does deleting in a singly list need the previous node?','To drop B in A → B → C you must set A.next = C. B does not store prev. A dummy node in front of head makes deleting the head the same code as deleting the middle.','d5'],
 ['How does a circular queue avoid shifting?','A fixed buffer plus front and size. Enqueue writes at (front + size) % capacity. Dequeue reads data[front] and does front = (front + 1) % capacity. Both O(1). list.pop(0) shifts and is O(n).','d7'],
 ['What is on the stack during fact(4), and what is the cost?','Four frames at the deepest point: fact(4) waits for fact(3), which waits for fact(2), which waits for fact(1) = 1, then 2*1, 3*2, 4*6 = 24. Time O(n), extra space O(n). A loop computes the same product in O(1) extra space. Python does not optimize tail calls.','d8'],
 ['Why is naive fib(n) exponential, and how do you fix it?','fib(n) calls fib(n-1) and fib(n-2), so the same subproblem is solved many times: T(n) = T(n-1)+T(n-2)+O(1) = Θ(φ^n). Memoize each n, or fill a loop from 0 to n, for O(n) time. Do not use a mutable default memo={}.','d8'],
 ['Why is insertion sort O(n) on sorted input and O(n²) on reversed?','The inner loop only shifts while arr[j] > key. On a sorted array that while never runs. On a reversed array each new key slides to index 0, so shifts sum to 1+2+…+n. Use > not >= so equals stay put (stable).','d9'],
 ['Why is merge sort always O(n log n)?','The tree has log n levels because each call halves the array. Every element is merged once per level, so each level is O(n). There is no already-sorted shortcut. Use <= on equal heads to stay stable. Extra space is O(n).','d10'],
 ['When is in-place quicksort O(n²), and how do you stop that?','When the pivot is always the smallest or largest (sorted input + last-element pivot), one side is empty and T(n)=T(n-1)+O(n). Randomize the pivot or take median-of-three. Recurse on p-1 and p+1, not p, or the pivot is partitioned forever.','d11'],
 ['When can you beat O(n log n) sorting?','When keys are not an arbitrary comparison: counting sort is O(n+k) for integers in 0..k; radix walks digits. If k is huge, counting is worse than n log n. Bucket sort is average O(n) only when values spread across buckets.','d12'],
 ['What is the binary-search invariant, and why lo = mid+1?','If the target is present it is in arr[lo..hi] inclusive. If arr[mid] < target, mid cannot be the answer, so lo becomes mid+1 (and hi = mid-1 on the other side). lo = mid can stall. Pair while lo <= hi with hi = len-1.','d13'],
 ['How do you get the first, last, and count of x in a sorted array?','bisect_left is the first index with arr[i] >= x. bisect_right is the first with arr[i] > x. Count is right - left. Last index is right - 1 when left < right. Use the half-open loop: while lo < hi and hi = mid, not the inclusive exact-value template.','d14'],
 ['What makes a binary tree a BST, and why is “left child < node < right child” not enough?','Every value in the left subtree must be < the node, and every value on the right > the node, recursively. A descendant can break an ancestor even when both children look locally fine. Inorder of a BST is sorted. Validate with a (lo, hi) range.','d16'],
 ['How do you delete a BST node that has two children?','Copy the inorder successor (minimum of the right subtree — leftmost there) into the node, then delete that successor from the right subtree. The successor has no left child, so its delete is a 0- or 1-child case. Predecessor (max of left) also works.','d17'],
 ['What is the difference between preorder, inorder, and postorder?','When you record this node: before both children (pre), between them (in), or after both (post). BST inorder is sorted. Recursive DFS uses the call stack. An explicit preorder stack pushes right then left so left is popped first.','d18'],
 ['Why is tree BFS a queue, and how do you get one list per level?','Level order is FIFO: enqueue children, dequeue the next node. For groups, drain exactly len(q) nodes — that length is the current level, frozen at the start of the for-loop. pop(0) or stack.pop() is the wrong structure.','d19'],
 ['When do you use a BST instead of a hash set?','When you need order: min/max, successor, first key >= x, or a range. Hash lookup is faster on average but unordered. Python has no stdlib balanced BST; bisect on a sorted list finds in O(log n) and inserts in O(n).','d20'],
 ['What are the three backtracking steps, and why unmark a maze cell?','Choose, explore, unchoose. The mark means “on this path.” Unmarking lets another path use the cell. out.append(path[:]) copies; appending path stores a reference that later pops empty. Grid keys are (r, c) tuples.','d21'],
 ['How is a heap stored, and what does the heap property actually guarantee?','A complete binary tree in an array: parent (i-1)//2, left 2i+1, right 2i+2. Min-heap: every parent <= its children, so heap[0] is the minimum. Siblings are unordered; the array is not sorted. Height is always log n because the shape cannot skew.','d22'],
 ['How do push and pop restore a min-heap?','Push: append the value (keeps the tree complete), then sift-up with the parent until the parent is <= the child. Pop: swap root with the last item, pop the end, then sift-down with the smaller child. Both O(log n). Peek is heap[0].','d23'],
 ['Why is heapify O(n) instead of O(n log n)?','Sift-down from the last parent to the root. Leaves are already heaps. Most nodes live near the bottom, so their sift costs 0 or 1, and the series n/4 + 2n/8 + … sums to O(n). n separate pushes sift from the leaves and are O(n log n). After heapify, only a[0] is guaranteed min.','d24'],
 ['When is a hash table the wrong structure?','When you need the next key >= x, a range, or repeated min/max — use a BST/bisect or a heap. Hash is for exact-key membership and maps, O(1) average. in on a list is O(n). Grid keys must be (r, c) tuples, not lists.','d25'],
 ['What happens when two keys hash to the same bucket?','A collision — normal. Chaining stores both in that bucket’s list and scans the chain. Open addressing probes later slots (CPython dict). Load factor n/m stays bounded by rehashing into a larger table. A constant hash makes one long chain, O(n).','d26'],
 ['When do you use an adjacency list vs a matrix, and what is a grid?','List is the default: O(n+e) space, neighbors in O(degree). Matrix is O(n²) with O(1) edge checks, for dense graphs. A grid is an implicit graph: cells are nodes, 4-dir in-bounds (non-wall) cells are edges. Always name both n and e.','d27'],
 ['How do you count islands, and why do you not unmark?','DFS from each unvisited land cell, mark in place or in a seen set, recurse 4-dir after a bounds check. One start = one island. Do not unmark: a cell belongs to one component. Backtracking unmarks because the mark meant “on this path.” Time O(rows*cols).','d28'],
 ['Why BFS for a grid shortest path, and when do you mark?','Equal-cost steps: FIFO visits closer cells first, so the first time you reach the exit is shortest. Mark when you enqueue or the same cell enters the queue many times. Multi-source: every start sits in the queue at dist 0. DFS is the wrong shortest-path tool.','d29'],
 ['How do you detect a cycle and topo-sort on an adjacency list?','Always keep a seen set. Undirected cycle: a neighbor already seen that is not the parent. Directed order: Kahn’s BFS from indegree 0; if you finish with fewer than n nodes, there is a cycle. Time O(n+e).','d30'],
 ['What four things do you state before writing a DP, and why is coin change not greedy?','State (what dp[i] means), base, transition, fill order. Coin change: dp[a] is fewest coins for amount a. Biggest-first greedy on [1,3,4] for 6 uses three coins; DP uses two 3s. Time is (states) times (work per state).','d31'],
 ['How does 0/1 knapsack’s inner loop differ from unbounded, and how is unique-paths filled?','0/1 walks capacity downward so this item is not reused. Unbounded walks upward. Unique paths: dp[r][c] = cell above + cell left; first row and column are 1. Draw the 3x3 table (corner 6) before coding. Independent rows: comprehension, not [row]*n.','d32'],
 ['What does n & (n-1) do, and how do you test a power of two?','n & (n-1) turns off the lowest set bit (12 is 1100 → 1000). Loop it to count bits. A power of two has one bit set: n > 0 and n & (n-1) == 0. XOR of a list where every value appears twice except one is that unique value. Python ~n is -n-1, not a 32-bit NOT.','d33']
];
let storageAvailable=true;
function cleanState(x){
  const base=EMPTY(); if(!x||typeof x!=='object')return base;
  for(const k of ['done','saved'])base[k]=Array.isArray(x[k])?[...new Set(x[k].filter(v=>LESSONS.some(l=>l.id===v)))]:[];
  for(const k of ['days','known'])base[k]=Array.isArray(x[k])?[...new Set(x[k].filter(v=>Number.isInteger(v)&&v>=0&&v<(k==='days'?7:cards.length)))]:[];
  if(x.notes&&typeof x.notes==='object')for(const l of LESSONS)if(typeof x.notes[l.id]==='string')base.notes[l.id]=x.notes[l.id];
  if(LESSONS.some(l=>l.id===x.last))base.last=x.last;
  if(x.theme==='light'||x.theme==='dark')base.theme=x.theme;
  return base;
}
let state=EMPTY();try{state=cleanState(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY)));}catch{storageAvailable=false;}
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s);
const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
function toast(message){$('#toast').textContent=message;$('#toast').style.opacity=1;clearTimeout(toast.timer);toast.timer=setTimeout(()=>$('#toast').style.opacity=0,2600);}
function persist(){try{localStorage.setItem(KEY,JSON.stringify(state));storageAvailable=true;}catch{storageAvailable=false;toast('Browser storage unavailable. Export a backup before leaving.');}updateStorageBadge();}
function updateStorageBadge(){$('.local-badge').textContent=storageAvailable?'● Saved on this browser':'○ Session only · export to save';}
function currentTheme(){if(state.theme==='light'||state.theme==='dark')return state.theme;return window.matchMedia&&window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches?'dark':'light';}
function applyTheme(){const theme=currentTheme();document.documentElement.dataset.theme=theme;const btn=$('#theme');if(!btn)return;const dark=theme==='dark';btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed',dark);btn.title=dark?'Switch to light mode':'Switch to dark mode';btn.textContent=dark?'Light mode':'Dark mode';}
updateStorageBadge();
applyTheme();
if(window.matchMedia)window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change',()=>{if(state.theme!=='light'&&state.theme!=='dark')applyTheme();});
const TRACK={python:{id:'python',view:'learn',label:'Python fundamentals',kicker:'01 · PYTHON FUNDAMENTALS',eyebrow:'PYTHON FUNDAMENTALS',sub:'Based on your NeetCode topic screenshots, with interview essentials added.'},interview:{id:'interview',view:'code',label:'Python for interviews',kicker:'02 · PYTHON FOR INTERVIEWS',eyebrow:'PYTHON FOR INTERVIEWS',sub:'The standard-library tools you use while solving interview problems.'},dsa:{id:'dsa',view:'dsa',label:'Algorithms & data structures',kicker:'03 · DATA STRUCTURES',eyebrow:'ALGORITHMS & DATA STRUCTURES',sub:'How the structures and algorithms work, from arrays through DP and bits. From the NeetCode beginners course.'}};
function trackFromView(view){return (Object.values(TRACK).find(t=>t.view===view)||TRACK.python).id;}
function trackOf(id){return (LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===id)||{}).track||'python';}
function inTrack(track){return LESSONS.filter(l=>(l.track||'python')===track);}
function viewOf(id){return TRACK[trackOf(id)].view;}
function displayNum(l){return String(l.num||l.id).padStart(2,'0');}
const days=[['Variables & operators','References, types, casting, arithmetic, Boolean logic','45–60 min','1'],['Functions & control flow','Parameters, scope, defaults, conditionals, truthiness','60 min','3'],['Loops & strings','Ranges, iteration, slicing, immutability','60 min','5'],['Lists & tuples','Mutation, shallow copies, sorting, nested lists','60–75 min','7'],['Sets & dictionaries','Hashability, membership, missing keys, costs','60–75 min','8'],['Input, exceptions & review','Read stdin, catch errors, revisit weak spots','30–45 min','10'],['Rest / buffer','Give the material time to settle. Catch up if needed.','Flexible',null]];
let filter='all',query='',sheetTrack='all',sheetQuery='',cardIndex=0,revealed=false;
function route(){const p=location.hash.slice(1).split('/');return {view:['overview','learn','code','dsa','sheet','plan','recall'].includes(p[0])?p[0]:'overview',id:LESSONS.some(l=>l.id===p[1])?p[1]:null};}
function go(view,id){const hash='#'+view+(id?'/'+id:'');if(location.hash===hash)render();else location.hash=hash;}
function title(kicker,heading,sub,tag='REVISION FIRST'){return `<div class="page-title"><div><div class="eyebrow">${kicker}</div><h1>${heading}</h1><div class="subhead">${sub}</div></div><span class="tag">${tag}</span></div>`;}
function lessonRow(l){const done=state.done.includes(l.id);return `<button class="lesson-row" data-lesson="${l.id}"><span class="lesson-num">${displayNum(l)}</span><span class="lesson-name">${esc(l.title)}<small>${done?'Revised · ready to revisit':state.saved.includes(l.id)?'Saved for another look':'Concepts, worked examples & common traps'}</small></span><span class="row-time">${l.minutes} min</span><span class="status-dot ${done?'done':''}">${done?'✓':''}</span></button>`;}
function overview(){
 const python=inTrack('python'), interview=inTrack('interview'), dsa=inTrack('dsa');
 const pythonDone=python.every(l=>state.done.includes(l.id));
 const interviewDone=interview.every(l=>state.done.includes(l.id));
 const dsaDone=dsa.every(l=>state.done.includes(l.id));
 const next=LESSONS.find(l=>!state.done.includes(l.id))||LESSONS[0];
 const percent=Math.round(state.done.length/LESSONS.length*100);
 const heroKicker=pythonDone?(interviewDone?(dsaDone?'ALL TRACKS · REFRESHED':'YOUR CURRENT FOCUS · DATA STRUCTURES'):'YOUR CURRENT FOCUS · INTERVIEW PYTHON'):'YOUR CURRENT FOCUS · PYTHON';
 const heroH=dsaDone?'Fundamentals, tools, and structures, refreshed.':interviewDone?'The structures behind the tools.':pythonDone?'The tools you will use in solutions.':'Good code starts with clear fundamentals.';
 const heroP=dsaDone?'Revisit saved topics, explain the rules aloud, and take your next step toward problem practice.':interviewDone?'Arrays through trees and backtracking — how the structures actually work.':pythonDone?'Sorting, deques, hash maps, heaps, and 2-D grids — revise these before the structures underneath.':'Pick up one concept. Understand the behavior. Build back the confidence, one session at a time.';
 const heroBtn=dsaDone?'Continue revising':interviewDone?'Start data structures':pythonDone?'Start interview tools':state.done.length?'Continue revising':'Start with variables';
 return title('A LITTLE SHARPER, EVERY DAY','Your coding comeback.','Rebuild the fundamentals. Get comfortable thinking in code.')+`
 <section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">${heroKicker}</div><h2>${heroH}</h2><p>${heroP}</p><button class="primary" data-lesson="${next.id}">${heroBtn} <span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></button></div><div class="hero-art" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rings"><div class="code-tile">&lt;/&gt;</div></div><div class="art-note">small steps. strong foundations.</div></div></section>
 <div class="stats"><div class="stat"><div class="stat-label">Topics revised</div><div class="stat-value">${state.done.length} <small>/ ${LESSONS.length}</small></div><div class="meter"><div style="width:${percent}%"></div></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="stat-label">This week’s sessions</div><div class="stat-value">${state.days.length} <small>/ 7 checked off</small></div></div><div class="stat"><div class="stat-label">Your learning rhythm</div><div class="stat-value">60 <small>min / focused day</small></div></div></div>
 <div class="grid"><div><div class="section-title"><h2>Build your foundation</h2><small>01 / PYTHON BASICS</small></div><section class="panel"><div class="panel-head"><h3>The essentials, in order</h3><button class="text-link" data-view="learn">View all topics →</button></div>${python.slice(0,5).map(lessonRow).join('')}</section><div class="section-title"><h2>Python for interviews</h2><small>02 / INTERVIEW TOOLS</small></div><section class="panel"><div class="panel-head"><h3>The tools used in solutions</h3><button class="text-link" data-view="code">View all topics →</button></div>${interview.slice(0,5).map(lessonRow).join('')}</section><div class="section-title"><h2>Algorithms & data structures</h2><small>03 / BEGINNERS COURSE</small></div><section class="panel"><div class="panel-head"><h3>How the structures work</h3><button class="text-link" data-view="dsa">View all topics →</button></div>${dsa.slice(0,5).map(lessonRow).join('')}</section></div><div><section class="side-card"><h3>A focused study session</h3><div class="session-line"><b>05</b><span>Recall yesterday<small>Explain it without the notes.</small></span></div><div class="session-line"><b>40</b><span>Revise one topic<small>Trace the worked examples.</small></span></div><div class="session-line"><b>15</b><span>Make it stick<small>Explain the traps. Note the gaps.</small></span></div><button class="text-link" data-view="plan">Open your study roadmap →</button></section><section class="side-card quiet-card"><div class="eyebrow">NO NEED TO RUSH</div><h3>Understanding first.<br>Problem practice follows.</h3><p>These notes are your first step. Use recall cards whenever you feel ready.</p><button class="text-link" data-view="recall">Explore recall cards →</button></section><section class="side-card"><h3>Need the short version?</h3><p>One card per topic: what to use, the trap, and the cost.</p><button class="text-link" data-view="sheet">Open the cheat sheet →</button></section></div></div>`;
}
function catalog(track){const meta=TRACK[track];return title(meta.kicker,'A refresh, topic by topic.',meta.sub)+`<label class="hidden" for="search">Search lessons</label><input id="search" class="search" type="search" placeholder="Search concepts, syntax, or topics…" value="${esc(query)}"><div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter lessons">${[['all','All topics'],['pending','To revise'],['done','Revised'],['saved','Saved for review']].map(([k,v])=>`<button data-filter="${k}" class="${filter===k?'active-tab':''}" aria-pressed="${filter===k}">${v}</button>`).join('')}</div><section class="panel" id="lesson-results">${filteredLessons(track)}</section><p class="muted" style="font-size:11px;margin-top:18px">A completion check means you’ve revised the topic. Revisit it until you can explain it without the notes.</p>`;}
function filteredLessons(track){const matches=inTrack(track).filter(l=>(l.title+' '+l.text).toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())&&(filter==='all'||filter==='pending'&&!state.done.includes(l.id)||filter==='done'&&state.done.includes(l.id)||filter==='saved'&&state.saved.includes(l.id)));return matches.length?matches.map(lessonRow).join(''):'<div class="empty">No topics match. Try another search or filter.</div>';}
function reader(id){const l=LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===id),siblings=inTrack(trackOf(id)),index=siblings.indexOf(l),done=state.done.includes(id),saved=state.saved.includes(id),meta=TRACK[trackOf(id)];
 return `<div class="reader-layout"><nav class="toc" aria-label="${esc(meta.label)}"><button data-view="${meta.view}">← All topics</button>${siblings.map(t=>`<button data-lesson="${t.id}" class="${t.id===id?'current':''}" ${t.id===id?'aria-current="page"':''}>${displayNum(t)} &nbsp; ${esc(t.title)} ${state.done.includes(t.id)?'✓':''}</button>`).join('')}</nav><article class="reader"><div class="reader-top"><span class="eyebrow">${meta.eyebrow} · ${displayNum(l)} / ${siblings.length}</span><span class="tag">${l.minutes} MIN READ</span></div><h1>${esc(l.title)}</h1><div class="reader-actions"><button id="complete" class="${done?'active-tab':'primary'}" aria-pressed="${done}">${done?'✓ Revised':'Mark as revised'}</button><button id="save-topic" aria-pressed="${saved}">${saved?'★ Saved for review':'☆ Save for review'}</button>${SHEETS.some(s=>s.id===id)?`<button data-sheet="${id}">Cheat sheet</button>`:''}<button id="print">Print / PDF</button></div><div class="prose">${l.html}</div><section class="notes"><label for="topic-note">Your notes & sticky points</label><textarea id="topic-note" placeholder="What surprised you? What would you want to revisit?">${esc(state.notes[id]||'')}</textarea><small id="note-status">${storageAvailable?'Autosaved in this browser. Export progress for a backup.':'Browser storage unavailable. Export progress to keep your notes.'}</small></section><div class="reader-bottom">${index>0?`<button data-lesson="${siblings[index-1].id}">← Previous topic</button>`:'<span></span>'}${index<siblings.length-1?`<button class="primary" data-lesson="${siblings[index+1].id}">Next topic →</button>`:'<button class="primary" data-view="plan">Back to roadmap →</button>'}</div></article></div>`;
}
function plan(){return title('YOUR SIX-WEEK PATH','A plan with breathing room.','Five focused days, one light review, one rest day. Adjust as your interview date becomes clearer.','60–75 MIN / DAY')+`<div class="week-grid">${[['01','Python refresh','References, control flow, strings, collections & the traps.'],['02','Interview Python & arrays','Sorting, deques, heaps, hash maps; then two pointers & windows.'],['03','Linear structures','Stacks, queues, linked lists & binary search.'],['04','Trees & recursion','Call stacks, DFS/BFS, BSTs & heaps.'],['05','Graphs & backtracking','Representations, traversals, dependencies & search.'],['06','DP & interview readiness','Intervals, greedy reasoning, DP basics & mock interviews.']].map(([n,t,d])=>`<div class="week-card"><div class="eyebrow">WEEK ${n}${n==='01'?' · CURRENT':''}</div><h3>${t}</h3><p>${d}</p></div>`).join('')}</div><div class="section-title"><h2>This week · Python revision</h2><small>${state.days.length} / 7 SESSIONS COMPLETE</small></div><section class="panel">${days.map(([name,desc,time,id],i)=>`<div class="day-row"><input type="checkbox" id="day-${i}" data-day="${i}" ${state.days.includes(i)?'checked':''}><label for="day-${i}"><strong>Day ${i+1} · ${name}</strong><small>${desc} · ${time}</small></label>${id?`<button class="text-link" data-lesson="${id}">Revise →</button>`:''}</div>`).join('')}</section><div class="section-title"><h2>Next · Python for interviews</h2><small>${inTrack('interview').filter(l=>state.done.includes(l.id)).length} / ${inTrack('interview').length} TOPICS REVISED</small></div><section class="panel">${inTrack('interview').map(lessonRow).join('')}</section><div class="section-title"><h2>Then · Algorithms & data structures</h2><small>${inTrack('dsa').filter(l=>state.done.includes(l.id)).length} / ${inTrack('dsa').length} TOPICS REVISED</small></div><section class="panel">${inTrack('dsa').map(lessonRow).join('')}</section><div class="section-title"><h2>The full roadmap</h2><a class="text-link" href="schedule.md" download>Download Markdown ↗</a></div><details class="reader"><summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:600">Read the complete schedule & study approach</summary><div class="prose">${SCHEDULE}</div></details><div class="section-title"><h2>Your data</h2></div><section class="panel"><div class="backup">Progress, notes, and recall confidence stay in this browser. Export a JSON backup to move them to another browser. Importing replaces the current progress.<br><br><label for="import">Import a progress backup: </label><input id="import" type="file" accept="application/json,.json"><br><br><a href="python-notes.md" download>Download the Python basics notes</a><br><a href="interview-notes.md" download>Download the interview Python notes</a><br><a href="dsa-notes.md" download>Download the data-structures notes</a><br><a href="cheatsheet.md" download>Download the cheat sheet</a></div></section>`;}
function sheetCards(){return SHEETS.filter(s=>(sheetTrack==='all'||s.track===sheetTrack)&&(s.title+' '+s.text).toLowerCase().includes(sheetQuery.toLowerCase()));}
function sheetResultsHtml(){const r=route();const matches=sheetCards();return matches.length?matches.map(s=>`<section class="panel sheet-card${r.id===s.id?' current':''}" id="sheet-${s.id}"><div class="panel-head"><h3><span class="lesson-num">${displayNum(LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===s.id))}</span> ${esc(s.title)}</h3><button class="text-link" data-lesson="${s.id}">Open lesson →</button></div><div class="prose">${s.html}</div></section>`).join(''):'<div class="empty">No rules match. Try another search.</div>';}
function sheet(){const r=route();return title('QUICK REFERENCE','The rules that matter.','One card per lesson. Open the full notes when a line is not enough.','CHEAT SHEET')+`<label class="hidden" for="sheet-search">Search cheat sheet</label><input id="sheet-search" class="search" type="search" placeholder="Search a rule, syntax, or topic…" value="${esc(sheetQuery)}"><div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter cheat sheet">${[['all','All topics'],['python','Fundamentals'],['interview','Interview tools'],['dsa','Data structures']].map(([k,v])=>`<button data-sheet-track="${k}" class="${sheetTrack===k?'active-tab':''}" aria-pressed="${sheetTrack===k}">${v}</button>`).join('')}</div><div class="sheet-jump" role="navigation" aria-label="Jump to a topic">${SHEETS.filter(s=>sheetTrack==='all'||s.track===sheetTrack).map(s=>`<button data-sheet="${s.id}" class="${r.id===s.id?'active-tab':''}">${esc(s.title)}</button>`).join('')}</div><div id="sheet-results">${sheetResultsHtml()}</div><p class="muted" style="font-size:11px;margin-top:18px"><a href="cheatsheet.md" download>Download the cheat sheet</a></p>`;}
function recall(){const [q,a,id]=cards[cardIndex];return title('OPTIONAL · ACTIVE RECALL','Can you explain it?','A low-pressure way to check your memory. No timer, no score, no coding problems.','REVEAL & REFLECT')+`<section class="recall-container"><div class="recall-controls"><small>Card ${cardIndex+1} of ${cards.length}</small><span class="tag">${state.known.length} marked confident</span></div><div class="recall-card"><div class="eyebrow">${esc(LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===id).title)}</div><h2>${esc(q)}</h2>${revealed?`<div class="answer">${esc(a)}</div><div class="filters" style="justify-content:center"><button id="know" class="${state.known.includes(cardIndex)?'active-tab':''}" aria-pressed="${state.known.includes(cardIndex)}">${state.known.includes(cardIndex)?'✓ Confident':'I can explain this'}</button><button data-lesson="${id}">Revisit the notes →</button></div>`:'<p class="muted" style="font-size:12px">Say your explanation out loud, then compare.</p><button class="primary" id="reveal">Reveal explanation</button>'}</div><div class="recall-controls"><button id="prev-card">← Previous</button><small>Confidence is self-reported.</small><button id="next-card">Next →</button></div></section>`;}
function render(){const r=route();const catalogTrack=Object.values(TRACK).find(t=>t.view===r.view);document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar nav button').forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle('selected',b.dataset.view===r.view));$('#crumb').textContent={overview:'Overview',learn:'Python fundamentals',code:'Python for interviews',dsa:'Data structures',sheet:'Cheat sheet',plan:'Study roadmap',recall:'Recall cards'}[r.view];if(catalogTrack&&r.id){state.last=r.id;persist();}$('#main').innerHTML=r.view==='overview'?overview():catalogTrack?(r.id?reader(r.id):catalog(catalogTrack.id)):r.view==='sheet'?sheet():r.view==='plan'?plan():recall();document.title=(r.view==='sheet'?(r.id&&SHEETS.some(s=>s.id===r.id)?SHEETS.find(s=>s.id===r.id).title+' cheat sheet':'Cheat sheet'):r.id?LESSONS.find(l=>l.id===r.id).title:$('#crumb').textContent)+' · Prep Studio';if(r.view==='sheet'&&r.id&&typeof document.getElementById==='function'){const el=document.getElementById('sheet-'+r.id);if(el&&el.scrollIntoView)el.scrollIntoView({block:'start'});}}
function toggle(list,value){const i=list.indexOf(value);if(i<0)list.push(value);else list.splice(i,1);}
document.addEventListener('click',e=>{
 const b=e.target.closest('button,a');if(!b)return;
 if(b.dataset.lesson){go(viewOf(b.dataset.lesson),b.dataset.lesson);return;}
 if(b.dataset.sheet){go('sheet',b.dataset.sheet);return;}
 if(b.dataset.sheetTrack){sheetTrack=b.dataset.sheetTrack;render();return;}
 if(b.dataset.view){go(b.dataset.view);return;}
 if(b.dataset.filter){filter=b.dataset.filter;render();return;}
 const r=route();
 if(b.id==='complete'){toggle(state.done,r.id);persist();render();}
 if(b.id==='save-topic'){toggle(state.saved,r.id);persist();render();}
 if(b.id==='print')window.print();
 if(b.id==='reveal'){revealed=true;render();}
 if(b.id==='know'){toggle(state.known,cardIndex);persist();render();}
 if(b.id==='next-card'||b.id==='prev-card'){cardIndex=(cardIndex+(b.id==='next-card'?1:cards.length-1))%cards.length;revealed=false;render();}
 if(b.id==='theme'){state.theme=currentTheme()==='dark'?'light':'dark';persist();applyTheme();return;}
 if(b.id==='export'){
  const blob=new Blob([JSON.stringify({version:1,exportedAt:new Date().toISOString(),...state},null,2)],{type:'application/json'});const url=URL.createObjectURL(blob),a=document.createElement('a');a.href=url;a.download='prep-studio-progress.json';a.click();setTimeout(()=>URL.revokeObjectURL(url),1000);toast('Progress backup exported.');
 }
});
document.addEventListener('input',e=>{
 if(e.target.id==='search'){query=e.target.value;$('#lesson-results').innerHTML=filteredLessons(trackFromView(route().view));}
 if(e.target.id==='sheet-search'){sheetQuery=e.target.value;$('#sheet-results').innerHTML=sheetResultsHtml();}
 if(e.target.id==='topic-note'){state.notes[route().id]=e.target.value;persist();$('#note-status').textContent=storageAvailable?'Saved in this browser.':'Session only. Export to keep these notes.';}
});
document.addEventListener('change',async e=>{
 if(e.target.dataset.day!==undefined){toggle(state.days,Number(e.target.dataset.day));persist();render();}
 if(e.target.id==='import'&&e.target.files[0]){
  try{const file=e.target.files[0];if(file.size>2000000)throw Error('size');const data=JSON.parse(await file.text());if(data.version!==1||!Array.isArray(data.done)||!Array.isArray(data.saved)||!Array.isArray(data.days)||!Array.isArray(data.known)||!data.notes||typeof data.notes!=='object')throw Error('format');if(!confirm('Replace this browser’s progress and notes with the backup?')){e.target.value='';return;}state=cleanState(data);persist();applyTheme();render();toast('Progress restored.');}catch{toast('That file is not a valid Prep Studio backup.');e.target.value='';}
 }
});
window.addEventListener('hashchange',()=>{const r=route();render();if(!(r.view==='sheet'&&r.id))window.scrollTo({top:0});});
render();
